    "lawCode": "Rpld §6 sub d ¶1, Emerg Ten Prot Act of 1974; rpld §26-511 sub c ¶13, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "Emergency Tenant Protection Act of 1974",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3770\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to repeal paragraph 1 of subdivision d of section 6 of section 4\nof the emergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-four, relat-\ning to individual apartment improvement rent increases; and to repeal\nparagraph 13 of subdivision c of section 26-511 of the administrative\ncode of the city of New York, relating to individual apartment improve-\nment rent increases\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo repeal the provisions allowing landlords to adjust rents in accord-\nance with the total cost incurred for individual apartment improvements\n(IAIs).\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 repeals paragraph 1 of subdivision d of section 6 of section 4\nof 2 chapter the emergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-\nfour, relating to IAIs.\n\nSection 2 repeals paragraph 13 of subdivision c of section 26-511 of the\nadministrative code of the city of New York, relating to IAIs.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe LAI increase allows a landlord to raise the rent on rent stabilized\napartment by passing the cost of physical improvements to the tenant by\nraising the monthly rent an amount equal to 1/40th or 1/60th of the\ntotal cost of the improvements depending upon the size of the building.\nThis increase can be taken in addition to any other rent increase to\nwhich the landlord is statutorily entitled and it is permanently added\nto the rent, even after the landlord has fully recouped the amount\nclaimed to be the cost of the improvement.\n\nLandlords are able to use the increase to move the apartment above the\ncurrent decontrol threshold that allows them to permanently de-regulate\nthe apartment. To approach that threshold more quickly, landlords can\nmake improvements that exceed what otherwise would be considered a\nreasonable cost. Although there are not absolutely clear government\nrecords of the frequency with which these increases are used, it is\nclear that rent regulated apartments are moving out of affordability at\na rate faster than statutory rent increases would allow.\n\nThe only present oversight mechanism is a tenant-initiated complaint.\nThe tenant may challenge the rent increase up to four years after the\n\nLandlord asserts that they incurred the cost of improvements. Only then,\nis a claimed IAI supported by adequate documentation.\n\nIAI changes are not justified in a system that permits landlords to\nraise rent at rates that far exceed the rates at which operations. And\ncapital improvement costs increase. They create problematic incentives\nfor landlords, and are difficult to monitor or verify.  This bill would\nrepeal the provisions that permit them.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act takes effect immediately.\n",
